  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...    \nLatest NBM/WPC guidance depicts the greatest coverage of Major \nHeatRisk across the forecast area on Thursday, with morning lows in \nthe middle to upper 80s across the lower deserts and forecast highs \n106F-110F. These temperatures during the hottest time of the year \nmay not prompt extreme heat headlines, but as we head into \nmeteorological fall, daily normals drop off noticeably, resulting in \nmore sensitive Major HeatRisk thresholds than what folks may have \ngotten accustomed to over the last few months. It also makes sense \nthat Thursday would be the warmest of the next several days, as \nthermal profiles would have had more time to respond to the near \nclimatological maximum strength ridge overhead. Heat indices will \nlikely also be at their highest values, peaking between 108F-117F \nacross the lower deserts. Temperatures then begin a gradual \ndowntrend heading into the weekend as the subtropical high weakens \nand is displaced from overhead. \n\nAs anticyclonic subsidence weakens, especially by late Thursday and \ninto Friday, chances for lower desert thunderstorms become \nintroduced across South-Central AZ (20-30%). A familiar monsoon \nthunderstorm evolution may begin to take place beginning Thursday \nand could last into the weekend, with afternoon thunderstorms \nfocused over the eastern AZ high terrain, which will push outflows \ntowards South-Central AZ in the evening that may be capable of \nsparking additional thunderstorms. Current thermodynamic profiles \nsuggest all the typical monsoon thunderstorm hazards may come into \nplay, including strong, gusty winds, blowing dust, localized heavy \nrain resulting in flash flooding, and frequent lightning. However, \nas we head into the weekend, the forecast confidence decreases \nsubstantially. WPC cluster analysis reveals large discrepancies, \nwith close to 40% of ensemble members comprising a solution which \nshows negative midlevel height anomalies diving into the \nnorthwestern and north-central CONUS, possibly impacting the flow \naloft as far south as the Desert Southwest and turning winds out of \nthe west, beginning a drying trend. Other solutions comprise \nclusters which show subtropical ridging remaining in place somewhere \nnear/over the Southwest US and keeping the influence of northern \nstream energy far enough north so as not to influence flow aloft \nsignificantly. Either way, drying will likely not be pronounced \nenough to preclude rain/thunderstorm chances once a sufficient \nforcing mechanism becomes introduced. Ensemble mean PWATs even \nremain near or above seasonal levels this weekend. Current NBM/WPC \nPoPs and QPF suggest higher/more widespread chances heading into \nSunday, perhaps picking up on the influence of a distant/elongated \nshortwave trough axis rotating through from the Northwest US \ntroughing that a good chunk of solutions show.",
